WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.000
Welcome back to Northwind lecture three. Today we look at the step size in gradient descent.

00:15.000 --> 00:32.000
Recall the update rule. We move the parameters opposite the gradient, and the step size scales how far.

00:32.000 --> 00:48.000
Here is a concrete example. Take the function f of x equals x squared, and start at x equals four.

00:48.000 --> 01:05.000
With a step size of 0.1 the gradient at four is eight, so the update moves us from four to 3.2.

01:05.000 --> 01:24.000
Keep going and the values shrink toward zero. Each step is a bit smaller than the last one.

01:24.000 --> 01:40.000
Now watch what happens with a step size of 1.1. The first update takes us from four to negative 4.8.

01:40.000 --> 01:58.000
The next one lands at 5.76. The distance from the minimum grows on every step instead of shrinking.

01:58.000 --> 02:15.000
That is the surprising part. A larger step does not mean faster progress. Past a threshold it diverges.

02:15.000 --> 02:32.000
A common misconception is that the algorithm is broken. It is not. The step simply overshoots the minimum.

02:32.000 --> 02:50.000
The threshold here is a step size of one, because the gradient of x squared is two x and two times one is two.

02:50.000 --> 03:10.000
So for this function any step size above one makes each update cross the minimum by more than it came.

03:10.000 --> 03:25.000
Next time we look at how to pick a step size when you do not know the function's curvature in advance.

